-- title:
--   Commutant of multiplication by a polynomial generator
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ and $A$ be commutative semirings, with $A$ an $R$-algebra. Let $x\in A$, and assume that polynomial evaluation at $x$ is surjective: every element of $A$ is $q(x)$ for some $q\in R[t]$. For $b\in A$, write $L_b(a)=ba$ for the $R$-linear multiplication operator.
--
--   For every $R$-linear map $T:A\to A$,
--   $$
--   \bigl(\forall a\in A,\ T(xa)=xT(a)\bigr)
--   \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
--   \exists! b\in A,\ T=L_b.
--   $$
--
--   Thus the commutant of $L_x$ consists exactly of multiplication operators, with a unique multiplier for each operator. This identifies the commuting linear maps when an algebra is generated by one element. The statement includes trivial algebras and requires neither a field nor finite-dimensionality.
-- source:
--   Derived supporting algebra lemma for the Senthil Kumar mission: if evaluation at x surjects from R[T] onto a commutative R-algebra A, an R-linear map T commutes with multiplication by x iff it is multiplication by a unique element of A. That element is T(1). The proof is polynomial induction, linearity and surjectivity. It is not quoted as a separate theorem of the source article. At Mathlib revision 0df444a360eaa60ab8c11dca51a86af692955474, Polynomial.induction_on' is in Algebra/Polynomial/Basic.lean lines 952-956 and Algebra.lmul is in Algebra/Algebra/Bilinear.lean lines 143-146. Primary documentation: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Polynomial/Basic.html and https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Algebra/Bilinear.html . R and A are commutative semirings; there is no field, finite-dimensionality or nontriviality assumption. No platform theorem dependencies or new definitions.

import Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.AlgebraMap
import Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Bilinear

theorem WeierstrassEllipticZeta.polynomial_cyclic_commutant
    (R A : Type*) [CommSemiring R] [CommSemiring A] [Algebra R A]
    (x : A)
    (hx : Function.Surjective (fun q : Polynomial R => q.eval₂ (algebraMap R A) x))
    (T : A →ₗ[R] A) :
    (∀ a : A, T (x * a) = x * T a) ↔
      ∃! b : A, T = Algebra.lmul R A b := by sorry
